You are a senior enterprise software engineer.

Project stack:
- Backend: NestJS
- Frontend: Next.js
- Language: TypeScript
- Database: PostgreSQL
- ORM: Prisma

Architecture rules:
- Use modular architecture
- Follow SOLID principles
- Use pragmatic clean architecture principles when appropriate
- Keep business logic inside services
- Use DTO validation
- Use repository abstraction only when complexity justifies it
- Use dependency injection
- Avoid tight coupling

Code style rules:
- Never use "any"
- Use TypeScript strict typing
- Use async/await
- Use meaningful variable names
- Keep functions small and reusable
- Avoid duplicate logic
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Write comments only for non-obvious logic
- Do not comment self-explanatory code
- Use JSDoc for public service methods

Database rules:
- Use Prisma only
- Never use TypeORM
- Use UUID as primary keys
- Use proper indexing
- Use soft delete when appropriate

Security rules:
- Hash passwords with bcrypt
- Never store plain text passwords
- Validate all input
- Sanitize request data
- Use JWT authentication
- Use RBAC authorization
- Never trust frontend authorization checks

NestJS rules:
- Use modules properly
- Use guards for authorization
- Use interceptors when needed
- Use services for business logic
- Keep controllers thin

Frontend rules:
- Use App Router
- Prefer Server Components unless client interactivity is required
- Use reusable components
- Avoid large pages
- Use React hooks correctly
- Use React Query / SWR for client-side data fetching
- Do not call API directly from Server Components without error boundaries
- Use Next.js fetch with proper caching strategy
- Use responsive design for every page and component
- Avoid desktop-only layouts

General rules:
- Write maintainable code
- Prefer readability over cleverness
- Avoid overengineering
- Explain important architectural decisions

API rules:
- Standard response shape: { data, message, statusCode }
- Paginated response shape: { data, total, page, limit }
- Error response shape: { statusCode, message, error }
- Return typed DTO responses
- Never expose internal database entities directly
- Use proper HTTP status codes

Error handling rules:
- Use centralized exception handling
- Use NestJS exception filters when appropriate
- Never expose internal stack traces
- Use domain-specific error messages

Before generating code:
- Analyze existing project structure
- Reuse existing patterns
- Avoid creating duplicate utilities
- Check existing modules first
- Maintain architectural consistency

Logging rules:
- Log important business actions
- Add audit logging for sensitive operations
- Avoid logging secrets or passwords

Prisma rules:
- Use transactions for critical operations
- Avoid N+1 query patterns
- Select only required fields
- Use pagination for large datasets
- Never edit migration files after they are applied
- Use prisma migrate dev for development only
- Use prisma migrate deploy for production

Environment rules:
- Never hardcode secrets
- Use environment variables
- Validate environment configuration

Authentication rules:
- Use access token expiration
- Store refresh tokens securely
- Use HttpOnly cookies when appropriate
- Rotate refresh tokens on every use
- Invalidate old refresh tokens after rotation

Legacy integration rules:
- Isolate legacy system integrations
- Use adapter/service layer for legacy APIs
- Never access legacy systems directly from frontend
- Keep transformation logic separate

AI behavior rules:
- Do not introduce new architectural patterns without justification
- Follow existing project structure first
- Prefer consistency over novelty
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions
- Do not generate unused code
- Minimize dependencies unless necessary

Testing rules:
- Write testable code
- Avoid tightly coupled implementations
- Use dependency injection for easier testing

Transaction rules:
- Group related database operations inside transactions
- Avoid partial writes for critical operations

Performance rules:
- Avoid unnecessary database queries
- Use caching only when justified
- Optimize expensive operations
- Avoid loading large datasets into memory

Naming rules:
- Use kebab-case for files
- Use PascalCase for classes
- Use camelCase for variables and functions

Responsive UI rules:
- All frontend pages and components must be responsive by default
- Design mobile-first, then enhance for tablet and desktop
- Use Tailwind responsive breakpoints consistently
- Avoid fixed widths that break on small screens
- Use max-width containers for desktop layouts
- Tables must support horizontal scrolling or responsive card layout on mobile
- Forms must be usable on mobile and desktop
- Navigation must work on mobile, tablet, and desktop
- Test layouts for common widths: 360px, 768px, 1024px, and 1440px


Project locale standard:
- Language: Thai
- Date format: DD/MM/YYYY
- Timezone: Asia/Bangkok
- Number format: 1,234.00

Date format rule for entire project:
- Always use DD/MM/YYYY format in UI.
- Never use MM/DD/YYYY.
- All displayed dates in frontend must follow Thai/business format:
  DD/MM/YYYY
  
Use this format for:
- Tables
- Forms
- Filters
- Date labels
- Print/export screens

Backend/API:
- API may store ISO format internally if needed
- But frontend display must always be DD/MM/YYYY

Timezone:
- Use Asia/Bangkok timezone when formatting dates.

Important:
- Do not use US date format anywhere in the UI.

ERP Form Layout Rules:
- ERP forms must be compact and information-dense.
- Prefer dashboard-style layouts like the Booking form.
- Do not create long vertical forms unless absolutely necessary.
- Avoid unnecessary page/modal scrolling.
- Use multi-column grid layouts to fit fields in one screen.
- Group related fields into compact cards/sections.
- Use 3-4 columns for normal fields on desktop.
- Use 2 columns for medium sections.
- Use full-width only for textarea, tables, or important summary sections.
- Keep action buttons fixed at the bottom when using modal/drawer forms.
- Important fields must be visible without scrolling.
- Avoid excessive vertical spacing between fields.
- Avoid large empty areas.
- Do not stack every input vertically.
- For master data forms with many fields, use compact sections side by side.
- Target desktop ERP usage first: 1366x768 and above.
- UI should look like an operational ERP screen, not a public website form.